<% content_for(:page_title) { "Follow a search — #{Netd.portal_name}" } %>
<div class="netd-page netd-faq">
  <h1>Follow a search</h1>
  <p>Be told when new theses and dissertations matching this search are added to the portal. No account is needed.</p>
  <table class="netd-about netd-api">
    <tr><th>Search</th><td><%= @query['q'].presence ? "“#{@query['q']}”" : 'All theses and dissertations' %>
      <% if @filters.any? %><br><%= @filters.join(' · ') %><% end %>
      <br><%= link_to 'See the newest matches', @results %></td></tr>
    <tr><th>Atom feed</th><td><code class="netd-feed-url"><%= @atom %></code></td></tr>
    <tr><th>RSS feed</th><td><code class="netd-feed-url"><%= @rss %></code></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>In a feed reader</h2>
  <p>Copy one of the feed addresses above into a feed reader (for example Feedly, Inoreader, NetNewsWire or Thunderbird).
    New additions appear as they are harvested, usually the morning after an institution adds them to its repository.
    Each item links to the thesis or dissertation on the portal.</p>

  <h2>By email</h2>
  <p>Feed-to-email services (for example Blogtrottr or Feedrabbit, or IFTTT and Zapier) can send you new items from the
    feed by email, as each item arrives or as a daily or weekly digest: paste the feed address into the service.
    These services are run by others and have their own terms. Email alerts from the portal itself are planned.</p>

  <h2>Other ways to follow</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Everything newly added: <%= link_to 'newest additions', search_action_path(sort: 'added') %>
      (<%= link_to 'Atom', search_action_url(sort: 'added', per_page: 20, format: 'atom') %>,
      <%= link_to 'RSS', search_action_url(sort: 'added', per_page: 20, format: 'rss') %>).</li>
    <li>Developers can use the REST API with <code>sort=added</code> or <code>updated_since</code>, or harvest the portal over
      OAI-PMH: see <%= link_to 'Developer Resources', developers_path %>.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
